    "elevatorPitch": "Most feedback advice focuses on how to give critique, but the real power plant sits entirely in the hands of the receiver. Mastering how you absorb, filter, and calibrate incoming information unlocks rapid personal evolution while shielding your sense of self.",
    "executiveSummary": "Human conversation operates like a home navigation system: you cannot calibrate your route if you refuse sensory input from the road. While leadership manuals exhaustively coach managers on how to deliver critique, Douglas Stone and Sheila Heen illuminate a hidden truth\u2014the receiver controls what gets through the gate. Whenever incoming data enters our ears, our nervous system instinctively deploys three defensive tripwires. We reject content as factually inaccurate (Truth Triggers), dismiss the messenger as hypocritical (Relationship Triggers), or panic that our competence is under fatal assault (Identity Triggers). When these tripwires fire, genuine data degrades into static noise.\n\nOvercoming this instinct does not require passive submission. Instead, think of feedback like pruning in a fruit orchard: you inspect each branch carefully, snip away the rot, and compost the scraps without uprooting the entire tree. Incoming feedback inevitably blends three distinct elements: Appreciation (recognition of effort), Coaching (direction for improvement), and Evaluation (ranking or assessment). Interpersonal train wrecks occur when we crave appreciation but receive coaching, or ask for coaching and receive a blunt evaluation. Disentangling these signals allows us to ask the right clarifying questions without derailing into emotional combat.\n\nUltimately, skillful receiving is an investigative science. Just as a driver relies on side mirrors to reveal vehicular blind spots that eyes cannot see directly, you need outside observers to witness your vocal pitch, nonverbal cues, and actual impact. By framing feedback as an experimental snapshot rather than an immutable verdict on your worth, you transform hostile critique into valuable navigational radar.",
