      "chapterNumber": 2,
      "headline": "The Three Flavors: Cheer, Coach, and Score",
      "keyInsight": "Imagine cooking a lavish feast for your guests, hoping for a warm smile and a compliment, but your guest immediately explains how you should have caramelized the onions differently. You wanted a warm hug, but you received a cooking lesson. Feedback comes in three distinct flavors: Appreciation, Coaching, and Evaluation. Appreciation says, I see your hard work and I value you. Coaching says, Here is a practical tool to help you sharpen your skills. Evaluation says, Here is where you stand against the standard. The quiet comedy of human life is that we constantly cross these wires. You ask for a coaching tip, but you get graded with a harsh score. Or you crave appreciation, but get handed an instruction manual. When wires cross, both sides leave the table angry and misunderstood.",
      "content": "Think of feedback like visiting an athletic gym. Sometimes you just need your workout partner to high-five you for showing up on a cold morning. That is Appreciation. It fuels your engine and keeps your spirit alive. Without it, you feel invisible and burn out quickly. Appreciation does not need a chart or a checklist; it just needs sincere, genuine warmth.\n\nOther times, you want a fitness trainer to adjust your elbow posture during a lift. That is Coaching. Coaching is not a verdict on your soul; it is simply guidance to help you improve and grow. But things turn sour when coaching gets disguised as a final report card. That final report card is Evaluation. Evaluation tells you where you rank, whether you passed the exam, or how you stack up against the benchmark.\n\nThe real trouble starts when people give one flavor while you are hungry for another. If you show a painting to a friend hoping for simple encouragement, and they spend forty minutes fixing your perspective lines, your heart sinks. Evaluation always drowns out coaching and appreciation because it triggers our deepest anxieties. To fix this, always clarify what kind of feedback you are asking for before the conversation even starts.",
