      "headline": "Trade 'You Are Wrong' for 'Tell Me More'",
      "keyInsight": "When someone offers advice that sounds completely wrong, our gut shouts: Dismiss it instantly! But wrong is a flimsy label that blinds us. When you say, That is wrong, you shut down your curiosity and start building a courtroom defense. Here is the trick: feedback is not a neat package of pure truth. It is a messy telescope looking at a tiny patch of your behavior. You judge yourself by your secret, noble intentions. Other people judge you solely by the visible impact of your actions. When someone complains about your habit, they are not describing who you are inside. They are reporting what it feels like to stand on the receiving end of your actions. Asking for more details does not mean you agree with them. It just means you are curious enough to look through their telescope.",
      "content": "Imagine you are backing your car out of a crowded driveway. A passenger gasps and shouts that you are about to hit the mailbox. Your immediate mental reaction might be defensiveness: I have been driving for twenty years and my mirrors are perfectly aligned! But shouting about your clean driving record does not change the physical position of the mailbox. What matters in that split second is finding out what specific angle they see that you cannot.\n\nWe all possess blind spots by design. You cannot observe your own facial expressions when you speak during a tense meeting. You cannot hear the subtle edge in your tone when you are exhausted at the dinner table. You know how hard you tried, but they only see the ripples you leave in the pond. When someone offers a bizarre piece of feedback, your goal is not to decide if it is right or wrong on the spot. Your goal is simply to collect data.\n\nNext time you hear advice that makes your blood simmer, replace your counterpunch with a flashlight. Ask two simple questions: Can you give me a recent example? What would a better approach look like to you? By asking for specifics, you convert vague complaints into concrete pictures. You are not surrendering or apologizing. You are simply studying the map before you decide whether to follow the route.",
